DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The header <_s_t_d_a_t_o_m_i_c_._h> provides type-generic macros for atomic opera‐
     tions.  Atomic operations can be used by multithreaded programs to pro‐
     vide shared variables between threads that in most cases may be modified
     without acquiring locks.

     Atomic variables are declared using the __AAttoommiicc() type specifier.  These
     variables are not type-compatible with their non-atomic counterparts.
     Depending on the compiler used, atomic variables may be opaque and can
     therefore only be influenced using the macros described.

     The aattoommiicc__iinniitt() macro initializes the atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t with a
     _v_a_l_u_e.  Atomic variables can be initialized while being declared using
     AATTOOMMIICC__VVAARR__IINNIITT().

     The aattoommiicc__llooaadd() macro returns the value of atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t.  The
     aattoommiicc__ssttoorree() macro sets the atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t to its _d_e_s_i_r_e_d
     value.

     The aattoommiicc__eexxcchhaannggee() macro combines the behaviour of aattoommiicc__llooaadd() and
     aattoommiicc__ssttoorree().  It sets the atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t to its desired _v_a_l_u_e
     and returns the original contents of the atomic variable.

     The aattoommiicc__ccoommppaarree__eexxcchhaannggee__ssttrroonngg() macro stores a _d_e_s_i_r_e_d value into
     atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t, only if the atomic variable is equal to its
     _e_x_p_e_c_t_e_d value.  Upon success, the macro returns true.  Upon failure, the
     _d_e_s_i_r_e_d value is overwritten with the value of the atomic variable and
     false is returned.  The aattoommiicc__ccoommppaarree__eexxcchhaannggee__wweeaakk() macro is identical
     to aattoommiicc__ccoommppaarree__eexxcchhaannggee__ssttrroonngg(), but is allowed to fail even if
     atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t is equal to its _e_x_p_e_c_t_e_d value.

     The aattoommiicc__ffeettcchh__aadddd() macro adds the value _o_p_e_r_a_n_d to atomic variable
     _o_b_j_e_c_t and returns the original contents of the atomic variable.

     The aattoommiicc__ffeettcchh__aanndd() macro applies the _a_n_d operator to atomic variable
     _o_b_j_e_c_t and _o_p_e_r_a_n_d and stores the value into _o_b_j_e_c_t, while returning the
     original contents of the atomic variable.

     The aattoommiicc__ffeettcchh__oorr() macro applies the _o_r operator to atomic variable
     _o_b_j_e_c_t and _o_p_e_r_a_n_d and stores the value into _o_b_j_e_c_t, while returning the
     original contents of the atomic variable.

     The aattoommiicc__ffeettcchh__ssuubb() macro subtracts the value _o_p_e_r_a_n_d from atomic
     variable _o_b_j_e_c_t and returns the original contents of the atomic variable.

     The aattoommiicc__ffeettcchh__xxoorr() macro applies the _x_o_r operator to atomic variable
     _o_b_j_e_c_t and _o_p_e_r_a_n_d and stores the value into _o_b_j_e_c_t, while returning the
     original contents of the atomic variable.

     The aattoommiicc__iiss__lloocckk__ffrreeee() macro returns whether atomic variable _o_b_j_e_c_t
     uses locks when using atomic operations.

BBAARRRRIIEERRSS
     The atomic operations described previously are implemented in such a way
     that they disallow both the compiler and the executing processor to re-
     order any nearby memory operations across the atomic operation.  In cer‐
     tain cases this behaviour may cause suboptimal performance.  To mitigate
     this, every atomic operation has an __eexxpplliicciitt() version that allows the
     re-ordering to be configured.

     The _o_r_d_e_r parameter of these __eexxpplliicciitt() macros can have one of the fol‐
     lowing values.

     memory_order_relaxed  No operation orders memory.

     memory_order_consume  Perform consume operation.

     memory_order_acquire  Acquire fence.

     memory_order_release  Release fence.

     memory_order_acq_rel  Acquire and release fence.

     memory_order_seq_cst  Sequentially consistent acquire and release fence.

     The previously described macros are identical to the __eexxpplliicciitt() macros,
     when _o_r_d_e_r is memory_order_seq_cst.

CCOOMMPPIILLEERR SSUUPPPPOORRTT
     These atomic operations are typically implemented by the compiler, as
     they must be implemented type-generically and must often use special
     hardware instructions.  As this interface has not been adopted by most
     compilers yet, the <_s_t_d_a_t_o_m_i_c_._h> header implements these macros on top of
     existing compiler intrinsics to provide forward compatibility.

     This means that certain aspects of the interface, such as support for
     different barrier types may simply be ignored.  When using GCC, all
     atomic operations are executed as if they are using memory_order_seq_cst.

     Instead of using the atomic operations provided by this interface,
     ISO/IEC 9899:2011 (“ISO C11”) allows the atomic variables to be modified
     directly using built-in language operators.  This behaviour cannot be em‐
     ulated for older compilers.  To prevent unintended non-atomic access to
     these variables, this header file places the atomic variable in a struc‐
     ture when using an older compiler.

     When using GCC on architectures on which it lacks support for built-in
     atomic intrinsics, these macros may emit function calls to fallback rou‐
     tines.  These fallback routines are only implemented for 32-bits and
     64-bits datatypes, if supported by the CPU.

